STEPS = ['Choose range', 'Search', 'Pause safely', 'Resume', 'Read report']
SCOUT_WORKFLOW = f'''<section class="section" id="session-map"><div class="section-top"><h2>Your GPU.<br>A scientific side quest.</h2><p>Choose when your PC is available for research. Scout’s saved-range and acknowledged-pause workflow lets you keep a search organised across sessions. Gaming and searching share hardware resources.</p></div><div class="session-map"><p class="eyebrow">Session map / select a stage</p><div class="session-stages" role="group" aria-label="Explore a Scout session">{''.join(f'<button type="button" data-session="{i}" aria-pressed="{str(i==0).lower()}" aria-controls="session-detail" disabled>{ICONS[i]}<span>0{i+1}</span><strong>{escape(title)}</strong></button>' for i,title in enumerate(STEPS))}</div><div class="session-detail" id="session-detail" role="status" aria-atomic="true"><h3 id="session-title">Choose an available GPU and a supported interval.</h3><p id="session-description">Use the official NVIDIA CUDA package and its documented range limits. A local range is your standalone search job; it is not a PGN network ticket.</p></div><p class="small muted">Interactive explanation only. These buttons do not run Scout, access your GPU, create checkpoints, or send reports.</p><noscript><p>Search uses your GPU. Pause must be acknowledged before treating the checkpoint as saved; compatible interrupted work can resume. A finished report still needs its verification and coverage context. Read the guide below for the full workflow.</p></noscript></div><p class="source">Based on <a href="https://github.com/wizz13150/prime-gap-scout#ranges-and-controls">Scout’s published range and checkpoint controls</a>. No promised gaming performance, power saving, or background scheduling is implied.</p></section>'''

RANGE_GUIDE = '''<section class="section split" id="range-boundaries"><div><p class="eyebrow">Read the map before a long run</p><h2>Big integers.<br>Exact boundaries.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Scout’s documented public domain is <strong>[2<sup>66</sup>, 2<sup>95</sup>)</strong>. The lower bound is included; the upper bound is excluded. That spans 67-bit through 95-bit integers. A bit width describes the integer, not the amount of GPU memory it uses.</p><div class="range-example"><span>67-bit integers start at</span><code>73,786,976,294,838,206,464</code><span>2<sup>95</sup> is outside the public domain</span><code>39,614,081,257,132,168,796,771,975,168</code></div><p>An interval may still fail a native planning constraint inside that domain: origin, boundary halo, low-64-bit window, descriptor count, or available memory. A refusal with a reason is different from silently skipping work.</p><p><strong>Positions are not discovered primes.</strong> A positions-per-second measurement counts the integer interval traversed under a stated policy. It is not a count of primality proofs, new records, accepted PGN tickets, or network capacity.</p><p><strong>Plan for your available time and storage.</strong> Begin with a modest supported job, understand its effective minimum-gap policy, and leave room for results, checkpoints, and optional diagnostics. Keep the package and session data together. Use acknowledged Pause when you need the machine.</p><p><strong>Match validation to your download.</strong> A source-tree validation note may describe an older build. Check the version and the package’s own records; compilation and host tests do not demonstrate physical execution on every GPU or operating system.</p><p class="source">Source: <a href="https://github.com/wizz13150/prime-gap-scout#exact-results-and-compatibility">Scout README: exact results and compatibility</a>, plus its range controls and validation documentation. Current release notes take precedence.</p></div></section>'''

READING_ROUTES = '''<section class="section" id="reading-routes"><div class="section-top"><h2>Choose your route.</h2><p>Start with a question. Each route points to a small set of sources with a different job.</p></div><div class="grid3"><article class="story"><span class="num">START / UNDERSTAND</span><h3>New to prime gaps?</h3><p><a href="learn.html#sieve-lab">Play the sieve mission</a>, then try <a href="#source-video-parker">Matt Parker’s visual explanation</a>. <a href="#source-primepages">PrimePages</a> adds definitions; watch its different composite-count convention.</p></article><article class="story"><span class="num">CHECK / INTERPRET</span><h3>Reading a result?</h3><p>Use <a href="#source-nicely">Nicely’s rubric</a> for certification and first-occurrence status, <a href="#source-records">maintained tables</a> for comparisons, and <a href="#source-oeis-gap">OEIS</a> for small record examples.</p></article><article class="story"><span class="num">BUILD / INVESTIGATE</span><h3>Looking under the hood?</h3><p>Read <a href="#source-combined-sieve">Troisi’s algorithm paper</a> for shared sieve work, <a href="#source-scout">Scout documentation</a> for that implementation’s boundaries, and <a href="#source-j0nn9">historical developer posts</a> for the evolution of a separate Gapcoin design.</p></article></div></section>'''
